# Outbound message copy

Slack, Telegram, and Pushover messages already have a shape. A new or changed message uses that shape. Confirm the exact text with the user before writing code.

## Match the current format

Find the formatter for that channel and that kind of alert. Copy its headline, field order, labels, markup, money and time formatting, and links. Do not invent a layout.

| Channel | Markup | Start here |
|---|---|---|
| Slack ops | mrkdwn. Header from `SOURCE_HEADERS`, then the body | `bot/services/slack_ops_format.py`, `format_slack_ops_message` |
| Slack escalation / union deposit / large cashout | Bold headline, `Club:`, group title in a code span, labeled fields | `format_*_slack_text` in `bot/services/escalation_notification.py` |
| Slack deposit alerts | `:bell:` title, `*name*`, Method / Variant / Week, conditions, dashboard link | `format_slack_message` in `bot/services/deposit_method_alerts.py` |
| Pushover | Plain text. `Player:` / `Amount:` / `Tag:` | `format_cashout_pushover_*` in `bot/services/staff_cashout_slack_reminders.py` |
| Telegram staff payment notices | HTML (`<b>`, escaped text) | `notification/formatting.py` |
| Telegram player copy | The `format_*_message` already used for that flow | `bot/handlers/`, `bot/services/` |

A new source gets a header in the same style as the table for that channel. Player-facing Telegram copy still follows `personas/player.md`.

## Confirm before coding

When the task adds or changes one of these messages:

1. Read the matching formatter.
2. Show the full message as it will be sent, with realistic values filled in. If an existing message is changing, show the current text and the proposed text.
3. Stop. Do not write the formatter, the call site, or tests until the user confirms that shape.

Skip the pause only when the user already supplied the exact copy in that message. Implement that text, still in the channel's markup.
